        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Foam, Rubber, Fiberglass or Wrap Tape: Which Pipe Insulation to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best pipe insulation for most homes is a closed-cell foam tube sized to the pipe. Four types cover almost every job, and the right one depends on where the pipe runs and what shape it is.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Polyethylene Foam Tubes</h3>

          <p>
            Gray or black polyethylene foam tubes, like the Tubolit, M-D, Duck and Frost King picks, are the cheapest and most common foam pipe insulation. They slip over the pipe through a pre-cut slit, and many have a self-sealing adhesive seam. They are ideal for long, straight runs of a single size in basements and crawl spaces.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Elastomeric Rubber (Armaflex)</h3>

          <p>
            Elastomeric foam, a flexible closed-cell rubber sold under names like Armaflex, bends around elbows more easily than polyethylene and is the standard for refrigerant and HVAC lines. It costs more per foot, but it is the most forgiving material for a neat, sealed job on water pipes too.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fiberglass Pipe Wrap</h3>

          <p>
            Fiberglass pipe insulation, sometimes called pipe lagging, comes as a strip you spiral around the pipe, usually with a foil or plastic vapor barrier. It fits any diameter and odd shapes, which makes it useful for mixed sizes and fittings, but it itches and takes longer to install neatly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Foam and Foil Wrap Tape</h3>

          <p>
            Thin self-adhesive pipe wrap tape, like the Frost King FV15, seals valves, tees and the joints between tubes. At about 1/8 inch thick it adds little insulation by itself, so treat it as a finishing product rather than the main layer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How to Size Pipe Insulation for PEX, Copper and PVC
          </h2>

          <p>
            Getting the size wrong is the most common reason pipe insulation fails. A loose tube leaves an air channel along the pipe, and a tight one splits open at the seam.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Nominal Size Is Not the Outside Diameter</h3>

          <p>
            Pipes are sold by nominal size, a name rather than a measurement. Half-inch copper, for example, has an outside diameter of about 5/8 inch, and the Armaflex listing says its 7/8-inch tube fits 3/4-inch nominal copper because that pipe measures 7/8 inch outside. Insulation is sized to fit the outside of the pipe.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">PEX vs Copper of the Same Size</h3>

          <p>
            Do not assume a PEX pipe and a copper pipe with the same nominal size take the same insulation. Much PEX sold in North America is made to copper tube size, but wall thickness, PEX-AL-PEX and metric pipe vary, and iron and PVC pipe (IPS) of the same nominal size are larger. That is why the M-D tube that fits 3/4-inch copper also fits 1/2-inch PVC or iron.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Measure</h3>

          <p>
            Wrap a strip of paper around the pipe, mark where it overlaps and measure the length: divide by 3.14 for the outside diameter. Or use calipers. Match that number to the insulation's inside diameter or the maker's fit chart, not to the size printed on the pipe.
          </p>

